<header class="report-header">
  <div class="header-badge">Orbital Intelligence Report</div>
  <h1 class="report-title">Active Satellites in Earth Orbit<br>2026 Catalog Analysis</h1>
  <p class="report-subtitle">A comprehensive data-driven analysis of 14,875 active spacecraft across all orbital regimes — covering constellation composition, orbital parameter distributions, and the recent mega-constellation launch surge.</p>
  <div class="header-meta">
    <div class="meta-item">
      <span class="meta-label">Data Source</span>
      <span class="meta-value">CelesTrak / NORAD TLE Catalog</span>
    </div>
    <div class="meta-item">
      <span class="meta-label">Epoch Range</span>
      <span class="meta-value">Feb 26 – Mar 30, 2026</span>
    </div>
    <div class="meta-item">
      <span class="meta-label">Total Records</span>
      <span class="meta-value">14,875 active satellites</span>
    </div>
    <div class="meta-item">
      <span class="meta-label">Audience</span>
      <span class="meta-value">Space-Industry Analyst / Policy Advisor</span>
    </div>
    <div class="meta-item">
      <span class="meta-label">Report Date</span>
      <span class="meta-value">April 2026</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<!-- ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
     MAIN CONTENT
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
<main class="container">

  <!-- ─── SECTION 1: Catalog Overview & Key Figures ─── -->
  <section class="section" id="overview">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-number">1</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">Catalog Overview &amp; Key Figures</h2>
    </div>

    <!-- KPI Grid -->
    <div class="kpi-grid">
      <div class="kpi-card">
        <div class="kpi-label">Total Active Satellites</div>
        <div class="kpi-value">14,875</div>
        <div class="kpi-sub">NORAD-tracked, all orbital regimes</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card leo">
        <div class="kpi-label">LEO Share</div>
        <div class="kpi-value">86.5%</div>
        <div class="kpi-sub">12,860 satellites — MEAN_MOTION &gt; 14 rev/day</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card starlink">
        <div class="kpi-label">Starlink Constellation</div>
        <div class="kpi-value">10,083</div>
        <div class="kpi-sub">67.8% of all active satellites</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card green">
        <div class="kpi-label">Median Inclination</div>
        <div class="kpi-value">53.16°</div>
        <div class="kpi-sub">Driven by Starlink's 53° shell</div>
      </div>
      <div class="kpi-card yellow">
        <div class="kpi-label">Launched Since 2020</div>
        <div class="kpi-value">13,461</div>
        <div class="kpi-sub">90.5% of entire active fleet</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-body">
      <p>The 2026 catalog captures <strong>14,875 active satellites</strong> with orbital element sets refreshed between late February and late March 2026, providing a near-real-time snapshot of the operational orbital environment. The data originates from NORAD Two-Line Element (TLE) sets distributed by CelesTrak and covers the full spectrum of operational spacecraft — from Cold War-era payloads still orbiting after six decades to the most recently launched Starlink shells.</p>
      <p>The single most salient feature of this catalog is the overwhelming dominance of Low Earth Orbit and, within LEO, the Starlink mega-constellation. These two facts reshape every statistical distribution in the dataset and must be held in mind when interpreting any aggregate figure.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Data Quality Notes:</strong> ECCENTRICITY values are near-universally very small (99.0% of satellites have eccentricity &lt; 0.01), consistent with operational circular or near-circular orbits. BSTAR drag coefficients and MEAN_MOTION_DOT are parsed from string scientific notation in the raw TLE format. EPHEMERIS_TYPE is uniformly 0 across all records, offering no discriminatory value.
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ─── SECTION 2: Orbital Regime Classification ─── -->
  <section class="section" id="orbit-regime">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-number">2</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">Orbital Regime Classification</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="section-body">
      <p>Satellites are classified into four orbital regimes using <strong>MEAN_MOTION thresholds</strong> (revolutions per day) derived from Keplerian orbital mechanics. The thresholds and their physical basis are as follows:</p>
    </div>

    <table class="def-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Regime</th>
          <th>MEAN_MOTION Threshold</th>
          <th>Approx. Altitude</th>
          <th>Additional Criteria</th>
          <th>Count</th>
          <th>Share</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="regime-dot LEO"></span><strong>LEO</strong> — Low Earth Orbit</td>
          <td>&gt; 14 rev/day</td>
          <td>~160 – 2,000 km</td>
          <td>—</td>
          <td>12,860</td>
          <td>86.5%</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="regime-dot MEO"></span><strong>MEO</strong> — Medium Earth Orbit</td>
          <td>1.1 – 14 rev/day (non-HEO)</td>
          <td>~2,000 – 35,000 km</td>
          <td>Eccentricity &lt; 0.3 or MM outside HEO band</td>
          <td>1,392</td>
          <td>9.4%</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><span class="regime-dot GEO"></span><strong>GEO</strong> — Geostationary / Near-GEO</td>
