    </div>

    <div class="section-body">
      <p>Constellation identification is performed by parsing <code>OBJECT_NAME</code> prefixes. <strong>SpaceX Starlink</strong> accounts for 10,083 satellites — 67.8% of the entire active catalog — representing a concentration of orbital infrastructure unprecedented in the history of spaceflight. The next largest single operator, <strong>OneWeb</strong>, manages 651 satellites (4.4%), less than 1/15th of Starlink's size.</p>
      <p>The "Other/Independent" bucket (2,796 satellites, 18.8%) encompasses a broad range of government, commercial, and academic spacecraft without standardised naming prefixes — including weather satellites, Earth observation platforms, technology demonstrators, and military reconnaissance payloads.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="callout danger">
      <strong>Statistical Skew Warning:</strong> Any mean, median, or distribution computed across the full catalog is heavily biased by Starlink. The fleet median inclination of 53.16° reflects Starlink's primary 53° orbital shell. Analysts should routinely segment Starlink vs. non-Starlink to draw policy-relevant conclusions.
    </div>

