      <h2 class="section-title">Launch Year Trends</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="section-body">
      <p>Launch year is extracted from the first four characters of the <code>OBJECT_ID</code> field (format: <code>YYYY-NNNXXX</code>). The resulting time series reveals a <strong>structural break around 2019–2020</strong> when SpaceX began mass-deploying Starlink. Annual launch counts of currently-active satellites increased from ~150 in 2019 to <strong>4,329 in 2025</strong> — a 28× acceleration in six years. The 2026 figure (962) is a partial-year count through late March.</p>
      <p>The cumulative active fleet grew from approximately 1,414 satellites at end-2019 to 14,875 by early 2026 — a <strong>10.5× increase in under seven years</strong>. This growth rate has no historical precedent and presents the most pressing challenge for orbital spectrum management, debris risk assessment, and international coordination frameworks.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="chart-card full-width">
      <div class="chart-title">Annual Launch Count of Currently-Active Satellites (2010–2026)</div>
