  <section class="section" id="conclusions">
    <div class="section-header">
      <div class="section-number">6</div>
      <h2 class="section-title">Key Takeaways &amp; Outlook</h2>
    </div>

    <div class="section-body">
      <p>The 2026 satellite catalog documents a space environment undergoing a rapid and historically singular transformation. The following takeaways are directly grounded in the data and carry direct implications for spectrum governance, orbital debris policy, and constellation licensing.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway-grid">
      <div class="takeaway-card">
        <div class="takeaway-icon">🛰️</div>
        <div class="takeaway-title">Single-Operator Concentration Risk</div>
        <div class="takeaway-body">SpaceX Starlink operates 10,083 of 14,875 active satellites (67.8%). A single operator's technical or business decisions — changes to constellation design, deorbit behavior, or frequency coordination — now have systemic consequences for the global orbital environment. Regulatory frameworks premised on a diversified operator landscape require re-examination.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="takeaway-card">
        <div class="takeaway-icon">📈</div>
        <div class="takeaway-title">Irreversible LEO Densification</div>
        <div class="takeaway-body">90.5% of active satellites were launched since 2020. The LEO shell between 530–570 km (Starlink's primary shell) has experienced a density increase unmatched in orbital history. Collision probability and conjunction frequency in this altitude band have increased commensurately, elevating the systemic risk of a Kessler-type cascade.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="takeaway-card">
        <div class="takeaway-icon">🌐</div>
        <div class="takeaway-title">Spectrum Congestion at 53° Inclination</div>
        <div class="takeaway-body">The 45–55° inclination bin contains 5,495 satellites — 37% of the active fleet — concentrated in a narrow orbital band. Ku/Ka-band downlink competition, inter-satellite link coordination, and ground-station time allocation at mid-latitudes are already constrained and will intensify as Kuiper and Chinese broadband networks grow.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="takeaway-card">
        <div class="takeaway-icon">🇨🇳</div>
        <div class="takeaway-title">Emerging Chinese Mega-Constellation Pressure</div>
        <div class="takeaway-body">G60/Qianfan, GeeSAT, Jilin-1, and other Chinese programs collectively represent ~609 active satellites, with stated constellation targets exceeding 10,000 spacecraft. The trajectory of Chinese broadband LEO deployment suggests a second major population surge comparable to Starlink's 2020–2025 growth phase is imminent.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="takeaway-card">
        <div class="takeaway-icon">⚖️</div>
        <div class="takeaway-title">GEO Remains Strategically Important</div>
        <div class="takeaway-body">Despite representing only 4.0% of active satellites (596 spacecraft), GEO remains irreplaceable for broadcast, weather, and military communications requiring coverage of fixed geographic areas without constellation overhead. The GEO arc is effectively full; any new GEO filing must displace or coordinate with existing operators.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="takeaway-card">
        <div class="takeaway-icon">🔭</div>
        <div class="takeaway-title">HEO / Legacy Heritage Fleet</div>
        <div class="takeaway-body">Only 16 satellites meet the HEO classification threshold (Molniya-type, eccentricity &gt; 0.3, MM 1.8–3.0 rev/day). The broader heritage fleet — objects launched before 2000 still tracked as active — numbers only ~115 records, underscoring that the active catalog is predominantly a post-2020 phenomenon.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout" style="margin-top: 24px;">
      <strong>Policy Recommendation Focus Areas:</strong> (1) Binding post-mission disposal rules for LEO constellations with quantified deorbit timelines; (2) ITU spectrum filing reform to prevent paper satellite filings that "squat" orbital positions; (3) Standardized conjunction data sharing requirements for all licensed operators; (4) Bilateral and multilateral coordination mechanisms for the Starlink/Chinese broadband overlap in the 530–570 km shell.
    </div>
  </section>

